1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,825
The Lane Was Probably
Named After My School.

2
00:00:03,825 --> 00:00:06,500
Neither Is There Anymore.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,819
I was born in 1960 on Jalan Payoh Lai,

4
00:00:09,899 --> 00:00:13,925
in what was then the
Kangkar area of Hougang.

5
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:16,763
Memory that old gets
slippery, so I can't be

6
00:00:16,843 --> 00:00:20,078
certain of every detail,
but I remember walking to

7
00:00:20,158 --> 00:00:23,275
school and to church
there for years afterward.

8
00:00:23,275 --> 00:00:25,902
The walk from the Upper Serangoon Road

9
00:00:25,982 --> 00:00:28,475
junction took about twenty minutes.

10
00:00:28,475 --> 00:00:31,924
My favourite shortcut ran
along a backlane to the

11
00:00:32,004 --> 00:00:35,750
left of Montfort School
called Holy Innocents' Lane.

12
00:00:35,750 --> 00:00:39,276
I only learned recently,
while researching this post,

13
00:00:39,356 --> 00:00:43,221
that the name traces back to
a cluster of Catholic schools

14
00:00:43,301 --> 00:00:46,975
that once shared that stretch
of Upper Serangoon Road.

15
00:00:46,975 --> 00:00:49,325
Montfort was one of them.

16
00:00:49,325 --> 00:00:52,233
Today, neither the lane nor the cemetery

17
00:00:52,313 --> 00:00:55,675
that once stood beside
it appears on any map.

18
00:00:55,675 --> 00:00:59,345
Sundays meant the same
walk in the same direction,

19
00:00:59,425 --> 00:01:01,900
except to Mass instead of school.

20
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:06,625
I'd cut through whatever backlane
shaved the most time off the trip.

21
00:01:06,625 --> 00:01:10,932
The church at the end of it,
Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary,

22
00:01:11,012 --> 00:01:14,105
was older than anything
else I knew as a child,

23
00:01:14,185 --> 00:01:17,425
though I had no idea how
much older at the time.

24
00:01:17,425 --> 00:01:22,611
A French missionary priest, Father
Ambroise Maistre, bought the land

25
00:01:22,691 --> 00:01:27,725
in 1857 for the Teochew Catholic
farmers and fishermen of Aukang.

26
00:01:27,725 --> 00:01:31,750
Many of the converts came
from Shantou in China.

27
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:35,700
The original attap chapel
eventually gave way to

28
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:39,729
the Neo-Gothic building
that still stands today,

29
00:01:39,809 --> 00:01:44,175
blessed in December 1901
and expanded again in 1933.

30
00:01:44,175 --> 00:01:48,400
It has been a national
monument since 2005.

31
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,876
It still holds a
Teochew-language Mass too,

32
00:01:51,956 --> 00:01:57,582
a tradition instituted in 1971 that
is, as far as anyone can confirm,

33
00:01:57,662 --> 00:02:02,375
the last regular Teochew
Catholic Mass left in Singapore.

34
00:02:02,375 --> 00:02:04,271
Montfort School, next door,

35
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:07,425
turned out to be part of that same tangle.

36
00:02:07,425 --> 00:02:10,926
It was founded in 1916,
not as Montfort but as

37
00:02:11,006 --> 00:02:13,340
Holy Innocents' English School,

38
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:18,325
established beside the church by
the parish priest of the time.

39
00:02:18,325 --> 00:02:23,050
The Brothers of Saint
Gabriel took it over in 1936.

40
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:26,065
It wasn't the only
"Holy Innocents" on that

41
00:02:26,145 --> 00:02:29,025
stretch of Upper Serangoon Road, though.

42
00:02:29,025 --> 00:02:33,773
Holy Innocents' Boys School grew
out of the same church grounds,

43
00:02:33,853 --> 00:02:37,775
and so did what's now CHIJ
Our Lady of the Nativity.

44
00:02:37,775 --> 00:02:41,977
Holy Innocents' High School
traces back further still,

45
00:02:42,057 --> 00:02:45,625
to 1892, and remains a
separate school today.

46
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:50,279
The names collided so often that
in 1958 Holy Innocents' English

47
00:02:50,359 --> 00:02:55,752
School was renamed Montfort, after
Saint Louis Marie Grignion de Montfort,

48
00:02:55,832 --> 00:02:59,900
the order's founder, specifically
to end the confusion.

49
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,938
I can't say for certain
which of those schools

50
00:03:03,018 --> 00:03:06,000
Holy Innocents' Lane
was actually named for.

51
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,557
Montfort, my own school, was simply one of

52
00:03:08,638 --> 00:03:11,275
at least three that could have claimed it.

53
00:03:11,275 --> 00:03:16,675
Montfort itself moved to
Hougang Avenue 8 in 1992.

54
00:03:16,675 --> 00:03:19,497
At some point after that, the lane and the

55
00:03:19,577 --> 00:03:22,825
cemetery next to it were
cleared away entirely.

56
00:03:22,825 --> 00:03:25,841
"Hougang" is the Mandarin
reading of the Hokkien

57
00:03:25,921 --> 00:03:29,275
and Teochew "Au Kang,"
meaning the end of the river.

58
00:03:29,275 --> 00:03:32,675
Kangkar itself meant "foot of the stream."

59
00:03:32,675 --> 00:03:35,377
That's where the settlements clustered,

60
00:03:35,457 --> 00:03:39,157
wherever Teochew planters
ran their kangchu system of

61
00:03:39,237 --> 00:03:42,875
jungle-clearing plantations
along Sungei Serangoon.

62
00:03:42,875 --> 00:03:45,964
There were roughly twenty such kangkars

63
00:03:46,044 --> 00:03:49,700
across the island by an
1849 newspaper count.

64
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:54,872
By 1986, more than nine in ten
Kangkar villagers were still Teochew,

65
00:03:54,952 --> 00:03:59,200
and the dialect was still
the area's everyday language.

66
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:04,050
The Kangkar I half-remember
was cleared in 1984.

67
00:04:04,050 --> 00:04:08,948
Its families were resettled into
three- and four-room HDB flats,

68
00:04:09,028 --> 00:04:13,150
and its land was folded into
what's now Punggol Park.

69
00:04:13,150 --> 00:04:18,053
Punggol End, further along, was a
different kind of village entirely:

70
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:22,900
Kampong Wak Sumang, a Malay fishing
settlement at Tanjong Punggol.

71
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:28,100
By one descendant's account
it was founded before 1819.

72
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:31,624
It only got piped water,
electricity and proper

73
00:04:31,704 --> 00:04:35,611
roads in the 1960s, and it
became briefly well known

74
00:04:35,691 --> 00:04:39,525
afterward for its seafood
restaurants and boatels.

75
00:04:39,525 --> 00:04:43,176
The government announced reclamation in

76
00:04:43,256 --> 00:04:47,275
1983 and told residents to vacate by 1986.

77
00:04:47,275 --> 00:04:50,969
The fishing port and
wholesale market outlasted

78
00:04:51,049 --> 00:04:54,261
the kampong itself, but even that finally

79
00:04:54,341 --> 00:04:58,275
closed in 1997, to make
way for Punggol New Town.

80
00:04:58,275 --> 00:05:03,203
Hougang New Town itself
was announced in 1979,

81
00:05:03,283 --> 00:05:08,400
planned for 460 hectares
and 120,000 residents.

82
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:13,787
Its first neighbourhoods were finished
by 1983, and the rest by 1992,

83
00:05:13,867 --> 00:05:18,225
the same year Montfort left
its original site for good.

84
00:05:18,225 --> 00:05:20,777
One thing about the area never got

85
00:05:20,857 --> 00:05:23,875
redeveloped along with everything else.

86
00:05:23,875 --> 00:05:28,033
Hougang has been held by
the same opposition party,

87
00:05:28,113 --> 00:05:32,850
the Workers' Party, in every
general election since 1991.

88
00:05:32,850 --> 00:05:36,820
It's a small, quietly remarkable
fact in its own right,

89
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:41,386
less a story about any one
politician than a marker of how far

90
00:05:41,466 --> 00:05:46,400
Singapore's electoral landscape
has matured since the kampong days.

91
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:49,170
I can't reconstruct the Kangkar of my

92
00:05:49,250 --> 00:05:52,100
childhood in any real detail anymore.

93
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:56,972
The specific smells, the sounds, the
neighbours' faces are mostly gone,

94
00:05:57,052 --> 00:06:00,400
the same way the lane and
the cemetery are gone.

95
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:03,915
What's left is the walk itself:
twenty minutes each way,

96
00:06:03,995 --> 00:06:08,537
a shortcut past a school that used
to have a different name, to a church

97
00:06:08,617 --> 00:06:12,725
that has outlasted every version
of the neighbourhood around it.

98
00:06:12,725 --> 00:06:16,251
Why it matters today: Most
of what made up the Hougang

99
00:06:16,331 --> 00:06:18,789
I grew up in — the lane, the cemetery,

100
00:06:18,869 --> 00:06:23,397
the kampong itself — exists now only
in scattered memories like mine,

101
00:06:23,477 --> 00:06:26,950
and in records like the
ones that let me write this.

102
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:29,851
Montfort School and
Nativity Church are the

103
00:06:29,931 --> 00:06:33,050
rare fixed points that
survived the clearing.

104
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:35,664
That's probably the only reason I can

105
00:06:35,744 --> 00:06:38,800
still find my way back
to any of it at all.
